    78	With `gamma=theta^(1/u_s)`, it then gives polynomials monic in `pi`, with
    79	`pi`-degrees
    80	
    81	```text
    82	u_s n/d_s,  u_s(-mu_1)/d_s, ..., u_s(-mu_{s-1})/d_s,
    83	```
    84	
    85	and Jacobian `-(u_s/b) gamma^(v_s-u_s-1)`.  The p.198 proof's last display
    86	puts the exponent in a denominator; the statement and Appendix-II monomials
    87	use the positive exponent.  This is the charged `ERRATUM[PROP63-JAC-SIGN]`.
    88	
    89	`SOURCE-READ`, p.198, Proposition 6.4 adds `u_s=1` and concludes
    90	
    91	> “the logarithmic radius `delta^*_{s-1}` of the minor disc
    92	> `D^*_{s-1} >= v_s/u_s = v_s`.”
    93	
    94	Its proof assumes the contrary, invokes Prop.6.1, uses the displayed gcd 1 to
    95	force all leading polynomials to be powers of a common linear polynomial,
    96	and says on p.199 that this “contradicts the very definition of
    97	`delta^*_{s-1}`.”  The gcd-to-linear step is expressly inside the `u_s=1`
    98	proposition.  There is no source permission to apply it when `u_s>1`.
    99	
   100	This is also how Appendix II announces its scope on p.207:
   101	
   102	> “We shall apply Proposition 6.4 to the first three cases. Note that in
   103	> these cases we always have `u_3=d_3-v_3=1`.”
   104	
   105	Thus the fourth case is deliberately outside that automatic descent.
   106	
   107	## 3. Moh's `(99,66)` case, exactly
   108	
   109	### 3.1 Tower and radius test
   110	
   111	`MEASURED / EXACT-ARITHMETIC` from the p.202 row and Def.5.1:
   112	
   113	```text
   114	n=99, m=66, s=3
   115	M_1..M_3 = (-66,77,97)
   116	d_1..d_4 = (99,33,11,1)
   117	V_2,V_3 = (8,8)
   118	v_3=8, u_3=d_3-v_3=3, v_3/u_3=8/3
   119	major (delta_1,delta_2,delta_3) = (4/9,1/3,-1).
   120	```
   121	
   122	The positive `delta_2=1/3` is the major-disc radius.  It is not the tested
   123	minor radius.
   124	
   125	`SOURCE-INFERRED, APPENDIX-II-DATUM`: p.209 introduces the distribution of
   126	roots in the minor disc `D_2^*` and immediately (“Namely”) displays its unique
   127	`pi`-root
   128	
   129	```text
   130	sigma = a_{-1} t^{-1} + a_0 + a_1 t + pi t^2,
   131	g(sigma) = g_sigma(pi) t^{-18} + ... .
   132	```
   133	
   134	P.147 says that a `pi`-root ending in `pi t^delta` is the general point of a
   135	logarithmic-radius-`delta` disc and that a disc uniquely determines that
   136	general point.  In the p.209 context this identifies `delta_2^*=2`.  This is
   137	extra source information about Moh's treated realization, not an output of
   138	the skeleton.  The order checks:
   139	
   140	```text
   141	ord g(sigma) = (n/d_3)(u_3*2-v_3) = 9(6-8) = -18.
   142	```
   143	
   144	Therefore `2 < 8/3`: **Prop.6.3 FAILS for the Appendix-II `(99,66)` case**.
   145	At skeleton-only resolution the same comparison had been undetermined.
   146	
   147	`D_2^*` uses the combined product, while p.209 classifies only `g_sigma`.
   148	Thus a `T_i^psi` root can witness separation in the linear branch even though
   149	the `g` leading polynomial has one root; these are not the same flag/series.
   150	
   151	### 3.2 The two alternatives and their actual dispositions
   152	
   153	P.209 says:
   154	
   155	> “The polynomial `g_sigma(pi)` is either a power of a linear polynomial or
   156	> the 9-th power of a cubic polynomial with precisely two roots.”
   157	
   158	| branch | source operation | mechanism | auditable outcome |
   159	|---|---|---|---|
   160	| A: `LINEAR-POWER` | p.209 transforms the data to `(n,m,M_2,V_2,delta_2,delta_1,Jac)=(27,18,21,8,-1,0,X^4)` and says the preceding method reduces the coefficient count to 10 | root count chooses one distinct `g_sigma` root; then coordinate/approximate-root coefficient reduction | `OPEN[99-LINEAR-10]`; no displayed contradiction |
   161	| B: `TWO-ROOT-CUBIC` | `g_sigma=H^9`, `deg H=3`, exactly two roots; Moh gives `Omega` and reduces to 11 variables | minor-disc leading-coefficient split, followed by a birational coordinate transform and coefficient reduction | `OPEN[99-CUBIC-11]`; no displayed contradiction |
   162	
   163	In branch B, the cubic's two multiplicities are `2+1`; raising to the ninth
   164	power gives `18+9` roots of `g` (`ARITHMETIC-INFERENCE`).  Moh's transform is
   165	
   166	```text
   167	x -> x^{-1},  y -> a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2+y x^3,
   168	Omega(f)=x^6 y^18+...,        Omega(g)=x^9 y^27+...,
   169	Omega(T_2^psi)=x^5 y^15+..., Omega(T_3^psi)=x^15 y^40+...,
   170	J(Omega(f),Omega(g))=x.
   171	```
   172	
   173	For A, p.209 says the preceding method reduces to 10 coefficients.  For B,
   174	p.210 says discussion of `Omega(g)=0` with a modified method reduces to 11.
   175	
   176	Neither alternative is excluded there by arithmetic, by a coefficient
   177	equation, or by a minor-disc count.  The explicit `a_9=0` / `a_9 != 0`
   178	coefficient contradiction on pp.210--211 treats
   179	`(n,m,M_2,V_2)=(15,10,11,3)`, the transformed `(75,50)` case.  It is not a
   180	calculation for either `(99,66)` branch.  P.211 finally says:
   181	
   182	> “All other cases can be computed directly as above. There is no
   183	> counter-example of polynomials of degrees less than or equal to 100.”
   184	
   185	That assertion of omitted computation is not an exhibited kill and cannot
   186	promote A or B to `KILLED` under `FALLACY-v2`.
   187	
   188	The exact `linear` versus `cubic^9 with two roots` classification, the
   189	transformed `M_2=21`, and the displayed `Omega` are case-specific assertions.
   190	Moh states no theorem making this pair of shapes a template for arbitrary
   191	`u_s>1`.
   192	
   193	## 4. The live `D=108` row
   194	
   195	### 4.1 What is and is not determined
   196	
   197	`MEASURED / EXACT-ARITHMETIC`:
   198	
   199	```text
   200	n=108, m=72, s=3
   201	M_1..M_3 = (-72,81,106)
   202	d_1..d_4 = (108,36,9,1)
   203	V_2,V_3 = (7,7)
   204	v_3=7, u_3=d_3-v_3=2, v_3/u_3=7/2
   205	major (delta_1,delta_2,delta_3) = (3/8,1/4,-1).
   206	```
   207	
   208	Def.5.1 determines the three unstarred radii.  Prop.6.1 determines only
   209	`delta_2^*>=1`.  No charged source gives this row a minor general point or
   210	actual root contacts.  Hence `delta_2^* >= 7/2` is **UNDETERMINED**, not false;
   211	substituting the major value `1/4` would be the forbidden major/minor
   212	identification.
   213	
   214	If an actual-root certificate gives `delta_2^*>=7/2`, Prop.6.3 applies and
   215	outputs `pi`-degrees `(24,16)` for `(g,T_1^psi)` and Jacobian
   216	`c gamma^4`, since
   217	
   218	```text
   219	u_3 n/d_3=24, u_3 m/d_3=16, v_3-u_3-1=4,
   220	```
   221	
   222	with `c != 0`.  This is `DESCEND-WITH-MODIFIED-HYPOTHESIS`, not a
   223	contradiction: the new Jacobian is monomial, and the source gives no direct
   224	elimination for the resulting pair.  In particular, no new `M_2'` or radius
   225	column is inferred merely from matching Moh's branch-A table.
   226	
   227	### 4.2 Faithful conditional branch enumeration
   228	
   229	Let `rho=delta_2^*` be the **actual** minor radius.  If `rho<7/2`, the general
   230	point `sigma_rho` has
   231	
   232	```text
   233	ord g(sigma_rho) = (108/9)(2 rho-7) = 12(2 rho-7) < 0.
   234	```
   235	
   236	Prop.6.1 therefore makes it a distribution detector.  Conditional on the
   237	common leading polynomial supplied by that actual disc, the degree-2 base
   238	`H` gives the only formal `u_3=2` root-count alternatives:
   239	
   240	| conditional subcritical branch | leading `g` profile | outcome and missing datum |
   241	|---|---|---|
   242	| A108: one distinct root | `H=c(pi-a)^2`, hence `g_sigma=C(pi-a)^24` (and the corresponding `f` exponent is 16) | `OPEN[108-A]`: Moh supplies no transform or coefficient contradiction; need the actual common jet, all `T_i` leading profiles, and the derived coefficient system |
   243	| B108: two distinct roots | `H` is two-rooted quadratic, `g_sigma=C H^12`; the two `g` packets are `12+12` | `OPEN[108-B]`: need the two subdisc jets and a case-specific transform/coefficient system; no source step kills it |
   244	
   245	This is abstract `ONE-G-ROOT / MULTIPLE-G-ROOTS` branching, not Moh's cubic.
   246	For general `u_s>1`, B must retain the full degree-`u_s` factor partition.
   247	
   248	A fixed radius-3 presentation shows why the radius matters.  Writing
   249	
   250	```text
   251	sigma_3=a_{-1}t^{-1}+a_0+a_1t+a_2t^2+pi t^3
   252	```
   253	
   254	for the whole minor cluster requires `delta_2^*>=3`; calling it the general
   255	point of `D_2^*`, as on p.209, requires equality `delta_2^*=3`.  Neither is in
   256	the skeleton.  If equality were measured, then `ord g(sigma_3)=-12` and the
   257	two profiles above apply.  In the two-root branch the formal analogue
   258	
   259	```text
   260	x -> x^{-1},
   261	y -> a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+y x^4
   262	```
   263	
   264	has multiplier `-x^2` and leading terms `Omega(f)=x^8y^16+...`,
   265	`Omega(g)=x^12y^24+...` (`PROVED-HERE, CONDITIONAL`).  It is no coefficient
   266	kill.  If `1<=delta_2^*<3`, this probe is unavailable.
   267	
   268	## 5. Typed `u_s>1` procedure
   269	
   270	Input is a screened skeleton `S=(n,m,M_i,d_i,V_i)` plus optional realization
   271	data `R` consisting of the actual minor radius/general-point jet and leading
   272	coefficient polynomials.  The skeleton alone is not silently enlarged into
   273	`R`.
   274	
   275	```text
   276	MINOR(S,R):
   277	  1. Check every source hypothesis: monic/total-degree condition, delta_s=-1,
   278	     M_s=n-2, the actual minor cluster, and u_s=d_s-v_s>0.
   279	  2. Compute v_s=V_s, u_s=d_s-v_s, q=v_s/u_s.  Compute Def.5.1 delta_i,
   280	     but label them MAJOR; never substitute delta_{s-1} for delta*_{s-1}.
   281	  3. If u_s=1, Prop.6.4 certifies delta*>=q.  Output
   282	     DESCEND-WITH-MODIFIED-HYPOTHESIS via Prop.6.3.
   283	  4. If u_s>1 and R has no actual delta*, use only Prop.6.1's delta*>=1.
   284	     If 1>=q, descend; otherwise output OPEN[MINOR-RADIUS].
   285	  5. If R proves delta*>=q, output DESCEND-WITH-MODIFIED-HYPOTHESIS with
   286	     Prop.6.3's pi-degree tower and monomial Jacobian.
   287	  6. If R proves delta*<q, form the general point at the actual delta* and
   288	     verify ord g<0 before using Prop.6.1.  Factor every leading polynomial.
   289	       A. One distinct g-root: output BRANCH A with the full T-root profile.
   290	       B. More than one distinct g-root: output BRANCH B with the full
   291	          multiplicity partition.
   292	     Apply only a case-proved coordinate transform and coefficient system.
   293	     If either is absent, return OPEN; a lower coefficient count is not a kill.
   294	```
   295	
   296	For `(99,66)`, p.209 supplies `R`, chooses radius 2, and asserts the exact A/B
   297	profiles.  For `(108,72)`, step 4 returns `OPEN[MINOR-RADIUS-108]`; §4.2 is
   298	the conditional continuation once `R` exists.
   299	
   300	## 6. Census application
   301	
   302	`MEASURED`: `census_usgt1.py` imports the two frozen charged programs,
   303	rechecks all receipt hashes, and applies
   304	`full_tree_polynomial_ode_ok`.  Group key is exactly
